11	326	261	587	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "The base verb of '手をつけていません' is 'つける'.",\n    "explanation_en": "In the expression '手をつける', the verb 'つける' means 'to attach' or 'to put on', but in this idiomatic phrase, it means 'to start' or 'to touch' something, often in the context of beginning a task or project. The phrase is conjugated into the negative te-form with 'ていません' to indicate that the action has not been done yet."\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "Another example of using '手をつける' is: まだ宿題に手をつけていない。",\n    "explanation_en": "This sentence translates to 'I haven't started my homework yet.' Here, '手をつける' is used in the same way to express that the speaker has not yet begun working on their homework. It’s a common way to talk about procrastinating or delaying a task in everyday Japanese."\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "N/A",\n    "explanation_en": "This field is not applicable as the question has been fully addressed in the previous answers."\n  }\n}	3	9	11	2025-08-26 10:56:15.768402	2025-08-26 10:56:15.768402
