34	337	271	608	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "clumsy → living a rushed life",\n    "explanation_jp": "「clumsy」は「不器用」という意味で、時間の余裕がない生活習慣を表すのには適していません。「living a rushed life」の方が「余裕のない生活」を自然に表現できます。"\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "like “ I barely made it!” → with thoughts like 'I barely made it!'",\n    "explanation_jp": "「like」の使い方が曖昧で不自然です。「with thoughts like」の方が、頭の中で考えていることを表すのに適切で自然な表現です。"\n  },\n  "change_3": {\n    "content": "even though I always want to improve myself → even though I always think about improving my habits",\n    "explanation_jp": "「improve myself」は少し曖昧で、文脈的に「生活習慣を改善する」という意味が伝わりにくいです。「improving my habits」の方が具体的に意図を伝えられます。"\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "I wonder why I can’t stop living a rushed life with thoughts like 'I barely made it!' even though I always think about improving my habits?"\n}	3	14	67	2025-09-06 05:11:33.531911	2025-09-06 05:11:33.531911
35	259	243	502	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "I always feel rushed when I have to catch the morning train.",\n    "explanation_jp": "「朝の電車に乗るためにいつも急いでいる感じがする」という文です。'rushed' は「急いでいる」「慌ただしい」という意味で、時間に追われている状態を表します。"\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "She looked rushed as she tried to finish her homework before the deadline.",\n    "explanation_jp": "「彼女は締め切り前に宿題を終わらせようとして慌ただしそうに見えた」という文です。'rushed' はここでは「急いでいる様子」を表し、余裕がない状態を指します。"\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "Even though I planned my day, I still ended up feeling rushed.",\n    "explanation_jp": "「一日を計画していたにもかかわらず、結局急いでいる感じになってしまった」という文です。'rushed' は「急かされている」「時間に追われている」感覚を表しています。"\n  }\n}	3	14	68	2025-09-06 05:14:33.793098	2025-09-06 05:14:33.793098
36	279	307	586	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "「今後もしばらくは」の意味と使い方",\n    "explanation_en": "The phrase '今後もしばらくは' (こん.ご.も.し.ば.ら.く.は) combines '今後' meaning 'from now on' or 'henceforth,' and 'しばらくは' meaning 'for a while.' Together, it conveys the idea of 'for a while from now on' or 'for the time being in the future.' It is often used to indicate a continuation of a situation or behavior into the near future."\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "例文: 今後もしばらくは、オンラインで会議を続ける予定です。",\n    "explanation_en": "Example sentence: 'For a while from now on, we plan to continue having meetings online.' In this sentence, '今後もしばらくは' sets the timeframe for the action of continuing online meetings, indicating that this arrangement will persist into the near future."\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "文脈での使い方",\n    "explanation_en": "In your reference text, '今後もしばらくは' is used to suggest that for the time being, the person with more time should handle cooking or hosting. It implies a temporary arrangement that will be revisited later, showing flexibility and consideration for each other's schedules."\n  }\n}	3	\N	69	2025-09-06 06:59:09.823604	2025-09-06 06:59:09.823604
37	308	155	463	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "今後もしばらくは → 当面は",\n    "explanation_en": "The phrase '今後もしばらくは' is slightly awkward in this context. '当面は' is more natural and commonly used to mean 'for the time being'."\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "しなくてはならないです → しなければならない",\n    "explanation_en": "The polite form 'です' is unnecessary after 'しなければならない', as it is already a formal expression. Omitting 'です' makes the sentence grammatically correct and consistent in tone."\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "当面は、毎日残業をしなければならない"\n}	2	15	70	2025-09-06 07:01:21.510338	2025-09-06 07:01:21.510338
38	382	333	715	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "友達によると、彼の妹が辛い料理を好きだそうです。",\n    "explanation_en": "This sentence means 'According to my friend, his younger sister likes spicy food.' It uses 'によると' to indicate hearsay, similar to your original sentence, and '好きだそうです' to convey that this information comes from someone else (the friend). '辛い料理' (からい.りょう.り) refers to spicy dishes, and 'を' is used with '好き' for grammatical correctness."\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "同僚によると、上司が新しいプロジェクトに興味があるそうです。",\n    "explanation_en": "This translates to 'According to my colleague, the boss is interested in the new project.' Again, 'によると' shows the source of the information, and '興味があるそうです' (きょう.み.が.あ.る.そう.です) reflects hearsay. This example demonstrates the structure with a different context (work-related) and a different verb phrase."\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "先生によると、クラスメイトが歴史の本を好きだそうです。",\n    "explanation_en": "This means 'According to the teacher, my classmate likes history books.' It follows the same pattern with 'によると' for hearsay and '好きだそうです' to indicate reported information. '歴史の本' (れき.し.の.ほん) means 'history books,' providing another variation in content while maintaining the grammatical structure."\n  }\n}	3	11	71	2025-09-06 09:32:37.424546	2025-09-06 09:32:37.424546
48	255	228	483	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "当面のところ、プロジェクトが終わるまで残業が続く。",\n    "explanation_en": "This sentence means 'For the time being, overtime will continue until the project is finished.' It uses '当面のところ' to indicate a temporary period, similar to '当面' in your revised sentence."\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "当面は、新しいルールに従わなければならない。",\n    "explanation_en": "This means 'For the time being, we must follow the new rules.' It mirrors the structure of your sentence by using '当面は' to set a temporary timeframe and 'なければならない' to express obligation."\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "当面は、旅行の計画を延期しなければならない。",\n    "explanation_en": "This translates to 'For the time being, we must postpone our travel plans.' Like your example, it combines '当面は' for the immediate future with the necessity expressed by 'なければならない.'"\n  }\n}	3	15	88	2025-09-09 23:03:40.195072	2025-09-09 23:03:40.195072
50	334	310	644	{\n    "change_1": {\n        "content": "果物狩りをしに行って → 果物を摘んで",\n        "explanation_en": "The phrase '果物狩りをしに行く' is unnatural in this context because it implies going somewhere specifically for fruit picking, like an orchard. Since it's in your own garden, '果物を摘む' (to pick fruits) is more appropriate and natural."\n    },\n    "change_2": {\n        "content": "アージアンレストラン → アジア料理のビュッフェレストラン",\n        "explanation_en": "The term 'アージアン' is a misspelling and should be 'アジア'. Additionally, specifying '料理のビュッフェレストラン' clarifies that it's an Asian food buffet restaurant, matching the intended meaning from the original language."\n    },\n    "change_3": {\n        "content": "そしてちょっと働きました → それから少し働きました",\n        "explanation_en": "While 'そして' is grammatically correct, 'それから' is more natural in a sequence of events like a journal entry. It better conveys the flow of 'after that'. Also, 'ちょっと' can sometimes imply a casual or dismissive tone; '少し' is more neutral and fits the context of describing work."\n    },\n    "corrected_text": "今日、庭で果物を摘んで、家の近くのアジア料理のビュッフェレストランに行って、それから少し働きました"\n}	3	18	90	2025-09-12 23:03:12.834279	2025-09-12 23:03:12.834279
51	299	86	385	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "今頃は → 最近は",\n    "explanation_en": " '今頃' typically refers to a specific time or season, often with a sense of nostalgia or expectation. '最近' is more appropriate for referring to 'these days' or a recent ongoing period."\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "最近はたくさん残業をしています"\n}	1	15	91	2025-09-13 10:48:26.457214	2025-09-13 10:48:26.457214
52	290	266	556	{\n  "answer_1": {\n    "content": "今頃 (いまごろ) は桜がきれいだろうなと思います。",\n    "explanation_en": "今頃 (いまごろ) can be used to refer to 'this time of year' or 'around this time,' often with a sense of nostalgia, expectation, or speculation. In this example, it implies thinking about how the cherry blossoms might be beautiful at this time of year."\n  },\n  "answer_2": {\n    "content": "今頃 (いまごろ) 彼はもう家に着いているはずです。",\n    "explanation_en": "Here, 今頃 (いまごろ) is used to mean 'by now' or 'at this time,' expressing an assumption or expectation about someone's current situation, like assuming they have arrived home by this time."\n  },\n  "answer_3": {\n    "content": "今頃 (いまごろ) になって後悔しても仕方ない。",\n    "explanation_en": "In this context, 今頃 (いまごろ) means 'at this late stage' or 'only now,' often carrying a sense of regret or realization that something should have been done earlier. It adds an emotional nuance to the statement about regret."\n  }\n}	3	15	92	2025-09-13 10:49:09.573682	2025-09-13 10:49:09.573682
53	312	261	573	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "いつも送ってる → いつも送っている",\n    "explanation_en": "The casual contraction '送ってる' is changed to the more standard and grammatically complete form '送っている' to maintain a polite tone consistent with the context of expressing gratitude."\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "お花の写真についていいことを言うの → お花の写真について優しい言葉をかけてくれて",\n    "explanation_en": "The phrase 'いいことを言うの' is rephrased to '優しい言葉をかけてくれて' to sound more natural and polite. Additionally, 'についていいことを言うの' is streamlined to 'について優しい言葉をかけてくれて' for smoother flow and to avoid the awkward use of 'の' at the end."\n  },\n  "change_3": {\n    "content": "ありがとう → ありがとうございます",\n    "explanation_en": "Changed 'ありがとう' to 'ありがとうございます' to maintain a polite and formal tone, aligning with the overall respectful nature of the sentence."\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "いつも送っているお花の写真について優しい言葉をかけてくれてありがとうございます"\n}	3	19	93	2025-09-14 03:07:40.171696	2025-09-14 03:07:40.171696
55	987	192	3014	{"change_1":{"content":"ピアスのプレゼントをあげた → ピアスをプレゼントした","explanation_en":"This rephrasing is more natural and idiomatic in Japanese, as 'プレゼントする' is commonly used to mean 'give as a present' instead of the slightly awkward 'ピアスのプレゼントをあげた'."},"change_2":{"content":"耳は空いていない → 耳に穴が開いていない","explanation_en":"The correct expression for 'ears aren't pierced' is '耳に穴が開いていない'. '空く' (to become empty) is incorrect; the proper verb is '開く' (to open), and the phrase needs '穴が' for accuracy."},"corrected_text":{"彼女にピアスをプレゼントしたけど、耳に穴が開いていない。"\t\t\t\t\t:{"content":"... → ...","explanation_en":"..."}}}	2	21	95	2025-09-15 04:41:02.989204	2025-09-15 04:41:02.989204
56	385	260	645	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "entitled with the name → rightfully called",\n    "explanation_jp": "「entitled with the name」は不自然な表現です。「rightfully called」の方が「名付けられるにふさわしい」というニュアンスを自然に表現できます。"\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "Cashew nuts bro → Cashew Nut Bro",\n    "explanation_jp": "固有名詞やニックネームとして使われる場合、「Cashew Nut Bro」のように各単語の頭文字を大文字にするのが英語の慣例です。"\n  },\n  "change_3": {\n    "content": "it convinced me that he’s → I thought it’s no wonder he’s",\n    "explanation_jp": "「it convinced me that」の部分を「I thought it’s no wonder he’s」にすることで、「さすがだなと思った」というニュアンスがより自然に伝わります。"\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "I made Kung Pao Chicken with Taylor last night. It was really funny that he was so excited about the cashew nuts part and I thought it’s no wonder he’s rightfully called Cashew Nut Bro."\n}	3	20	97	2025-09-15 04:42:04.647831	2025-09-15 04:42:04.647831
57	315	194	509	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "No wonder I get muscle pain → No wonder I have muscle pain",\n    "explanation_jp": "「get」を「have」に変更することで、筋肉痛がすでに存在する状態をより自然に表現できます。日本語の「全身筋肉痛になっても」に合うように、現在形で状態を表す方が適切です。"\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "as I rarely do exercise → because I rarely exercise",\n    "explanation_jp": "「as」を「because」に変えることで、理由をより明確に示すことができます。英語ではこの文脈で「because」の方が自然で、因果関係が強調されます。また、「do exercise」を「exercise」に簡略化し、自然な表現にしました。"\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "No wonder I have muscle pain all over my body because I rarely exercise"\n}	2	20	99	2025-09-19 11:54:20.03514	2025-09-19 11:54:20.03514
58	315	254	569	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "No wonder I get muscle pain → It's no surprise that I have muscle pain",\n    "explanation_jp": "「No wonder」は文法的には正しいが、「It's no surprise that」の方が自然で、口語的なニュアンスが合う。また、「get」を「have」に変えることで、状態を強調し、より自然な表現になる"\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "all over my body → throughout my entire body",\n    "explanation_jp": "「all over my body」も正しいが、「throughout my entire body」の方がややフォーマルで、筋肉痛の広がりをより明確に表現できる"\n  },\n  "change_3": {\n    "content": "as I rarely do exercise → since I rarely exercise",\n    "explanation_jp": "「do exercise」は文法的には正しいが、「exercise」だけを動詞として使うのがより自然。また、「as」を「since」に変えることで、因果関係がより明確になる"\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "It's no surprise that I have muscle pain throughout my entire body since I rarely exercise"\n}	3	20	100	2025-09-19 11:54:26.399433	2025-09-19 11:54:26.399433
59	330	234	564	{\n  "change_1": {\n    "content": "I went for futsal → I went to play futsal",\n    "explanation_jp": "「went for futsal」は不自然な表現です。「went to play futsal」の方が自然で、フットサルをしに行ったという意味が明確になります。"\n  },\n  "change_2": {\n    "content": "in Toyosu → at Toyosu",\n    "explanation_jp": "場所を表す場合、特定のエリアや施設では「at」を使うのが一般的です。「in」よりも「at Toyosu」の方が適切です。"\n  },\n  "change_3": {\n    "content": "orthodontic department → the orthodontics department",\n    "explanation_jp": "特定の部署や部門を指す場合、通常は「the」を付けて具体性を出します。「orthodontic department」よりも「the orthodontics department」が自然です。"\n  },\n  "corrected_text": "I went to play futsal yesterday at Toyosu with my classmates and some doctors from the orthodontics department, and I had so much fun!"\n}	3	23	101	2025-09-19 12:03:34.217225	2025-09-19 12:03:34.217225
